WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.275
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.275 --> 00:00:12.880
It's crucial for leaders today to be able
to communicate effectively.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:18.000
If as a leader you want to influence the
direction of an organization, or

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.130
the output of your employees and your team
members.

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:25.485
The best tool in your tool kit as a
leader, is effective and

00:00:25.485 --> 00:00:26.861
clear communication.

00:00:26.861 --> 00:00:29.965
[MUSIC]

00:00:29.965 --> 00:00:35.174
The first place I being when I work with a
leader who is trying to communicate more

00:00:35.174 --> 00:00:39.607
effectively, is to teach a very simple
framework that allows you to

00:00:39.607 --> 00:00:44.200
create a strategy, before you ever write,
or ever speak.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.160
And the heart of that strategy is just the
word aim.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.520
Who is my audience?

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:49.920
What is my intent?

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.730
And what is my message?

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:54.720
If, if a leader can think through
audience, intent, and

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.390
message, as they're creating an email, or
a Tweet, or a report, or

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:05.620
a presentation and really distill within
each of those three pieces what

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:09.280
is it they want to achieve with the
audience that they're speaking to.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.940
That's the first place to become more
effective as a leader who communicates.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:16.210
[MUSIC]

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:21.330
The more I know about the group of people
that I am trying to communicate with.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:23.390
What inspires them to action?

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:24.570
Where do they gather?

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.350
What resources do they rely on?

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:29.480
What sources of news do they read?

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.860
What are their peer groups of influence?

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:37.590
The more I understand the audience, the
more I can decide what's the best vehicle

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:42.660
or multiple vehicles that I might want to
use in order to communicate with them.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.020
When I'm only communicating with one
person, and

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.770
I'm doing it interpersonally with just two
people sitting in a room,

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:53.440
I have the full benefit of both the verbal
and

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.140
the non-verbal communication between me
and that other person.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:01.290
As a leader, I don't have that freedom
where every message I deliver,

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.330
I can deliver one on one.

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:06.550
So as I start communicating to a small
group of people,

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:09.390
a larger group of people, a very large
group or

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:14.890
a mass audience, I have to also expand the
different vehicles that I might use.

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:18.362
Choosing the vehicle or vehicles that
gonna be most effective,

00:02:18.362 --> 00:02:21.008
based on the audience analysis that I've
done.

00:02:21.008 --> 00:02:24.311
[MUSIC]

00:02:24.311 --> 00:02:27.949
Any time that a leader is standing up in
front of a group of people to

00:02:27.949 --> 00:02:32.670
deliver a message, there are three aspects
to that communication.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:35.460
There's the verbal, which are the actual
words that,

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.380
that person says to the audience.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:41.100
If I was to do a transcript of everything
that was said,

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.350
that's the verbal component of the
communication.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:48.690
There's also the vocal component of
communication, how loud or

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:54.020
how soft, how much I use pauses for
emphasis, or how rapid

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:57.250
I deliver to show a sense of excitement
and enthusiasm for what's coming.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:59.960
And that's the vocal aspect of
communication.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:04.090
But the third and, many researchers argue,

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:09.420
the most powerful aspect of communication
is the visual,

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:14.890
the nonverbal communication, my sense of
gestures, eye contact, movement.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:18.282
Even how I'm dressed and the posture that
I use,

00:03:18.282 --> 00:03:22.937
all is a part of my presence as a leader
in front of a group of people.

00:03:22.937 --> 00:03:28.157
So when the visual, the vocal and the
verbal all come together effectively,

00:03:28.157 --> 00:03:32.170
then a leader is able to have a sense of
executive presence,

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:35.461
as they deliver a presentation, share a
story or

00:03:35.461 --> 00:03:39.420
have a conversation with the teams that
they are leading.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:49.279
[MUSIC]

